/**

@page page_installation_with_docker Installation with Docker

Here we maintain Dockerfiles for different compilers.
They reuse the Spack environments stored under `utilities/spack`.

## Usage

<div style="color:Black ; background-color: #ccf ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;">
\par 🍎 Running on Apple Silicon (ARM) Macs
Our images target `linux/amd64`.
Docker Desktop runs them on M-series Macs through its built-in emulation.
Add `--platform linux/amd64` to the `docker run` command:
```shell
docker run --platform linux/amd64 -it -v ${PWD}:/work --rm --privileged peanoframework/<compiler>
```
</div>

We recommend that users pull the prebuilt images from [Dockerhub](https://hub.docker.com/u/peanoframework).
The images `gcc`, `intel`, `nvidia`, `llvm` and `amd` contain the same dependencies to build, run and visualise Peano applications.
Spack installs every dependency of the images.
Each image adds one compiler and one MPI library.
Both support GPU offloading.
Pull the image whose compiler fits your hardware.

Only the image builds use the images `base` and `spack`.

The `gcc` and `llvm` images also contain Kokkos for `sm_75`.
`sm_75` is the oldest architecture that CUDA 13 supports.
The Peano build links this Kokkos.
On a newer GPU, the driver compiles the embedded PTX at startup.
Kokkos then prints a warning.
The `intel` image contains Kokkos for SYCL.
The `amd` image contains Kokkos for `gfx90a`.
The `llvm`, `intel` and `amd` images also contain AdaptiveCpp for CUDA, Level Zero and HIP respectively.
AdaptiveCpp compiles device code with its own LLVM 19.
In the `llvm` image, AdaptiveCpp also uses its own CUDA 12.
AdaptiveCpp 25.10 supports LLVM up to version 20 and CUDA up to version 12.
AdaptiveCpp selects the GPU architecture when you build Peano.
In the `intel` image, Peano builds with AdaptiveCpp only when `/opt/views/acpp/bin/clang` and
`/opt/views/acpp/bin/clang++` are the C and C++ compilers.

To use a prebuilt image, clone Peano, change into the checkout and start the container with the checkout mounted at `/work`:

```shell
docker run -it -v ${PWD}:/work --rm --privileged peanoframework/<compiler>
```

### With GPU Support

```shell
docker run -it -v ${PWD}:/work --rm --gpus=all --privileged peanoframework/<compiler>
```

### With Port Forwarding (JupyterLab)

```shell
docker run -it -v ${PWD}:/work -p 8888:8888 --rm --privileged peanoframework/<compiler>
```

Then start JupyterLab inside the container:

```shell
jupyter lab --allow-root --port=8888 --no-browser --ip=0.0.0.0
```

## Build (for Developers)

This section addresses developers who change or add dependencies of the Docker images.
An image build takes hours and requires root access.

Run the following commands in the Peano root directory to build the Docker images.

### Building Base Images

```shell
docker build -t peanoframework/base -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.base .
docker build -t peanoframework/spack -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.spack .
```

### Building Compiler Images

```shell
docker build -t peanoframework/gcc -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.gcc .
docker build -t peanoframework/intel -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.intel .
docker build -t peanoframework/nvidia -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.nvidia .
docker build -t peanoframework/amd -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.amd .
docker build -t peanoframework/llvm -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.llvm .
```

### Building Extension Images

```shell
docker build -t peanoframework/exahype2 -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.exahype2 .
docker build -t peanoframework/exahype2-tutorials -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.exahype2-tutorials .
```

*/
